        "Casimir died on March 4, 1484 AD, at Grodno, in present-day Belarus, at the age of twenty-five. He had suffered from tuberculosis for some time, and his health deteriorated despite attempts to treat his illness. He accepted his approaching death with great faith and continued to pray and encourage those around him. His body was taken to Vilnius and buried in the Cathedral of St. Stanislaus, where his tomb soon became a place of pilgrimage and devotion. His early death contributed to the growth of his reputation for holiness, as those who had known him remembered his extraordinary piety despite his young age and royal position.",

      legacy:
        "St. Casimir is remembered as one of the most prominent examples of youthful holiness in the Catholic Church. He demonstrated that wealth, political influence, and royal status need not become obstacles to holiness when they are placed at the service of God and neighbour. His devotion to the Blessed Virgin Mary became an important part of his spiritual legacy, and he was traditionally associated with the Marian hymn 'Omni Die Dic Mariae,' although his authorship of the hymn is uncertain. Devotion to Casimir spread throughout Lithuania, Poland, and the wider Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth. He became particularly associated with young people, students, and those seeking to live faithfully in the midst of worldly responsibilities. His relics remain venerated in Vilnius Cathedral, and he is regarded as one of the principal patron saints of Lithuania and a powerful symbol of the country's Catholic heritage.",

      canonization: "Casimir was canonized by Pope Clement VIII in 1602 AD.",
