    patronOf:
      "Poor Clares, widows, expectant mothers, and those seeking reform in religious life",

    longDescription: {
      earlyLife:
        "Colette Boylet was born Nicolette Boellet on January 13, 1381 AD, in Corbie, Picardy, France. Her parents, Robert Boellet and Marguerite Moyon, were devout Catholics, and her father served as a carpenter and administrator at the Benedictine Abbey of Corbie. According to tradition, her parents had prayed for a child in their later years before her birth. From an early age, Colette showed an unusual attraction to prayer, fasting, and acts of charity. She was known for giving away food and clothing to the poor despite her own family's modest circumstances. After the death of both her parents when she was seventeen, she sought a religious vocation and initially joined several different religious communities before finding the form of life to which she felt called.",

      spiritualLife:
        "Colette eventually became a member of the Third Order of St. Francis and, under the guidance of the Franciscan friar Father Henry de Baume, embraced an exceptionally austere life of prayer and penance. In 1402 AD, she became an enclosed anchoress, living in a small cell attached to the Church of Saint-Germain in Corbie. She remained there for several years, dedicating herself almost entirely to prayer and contemplation. Colette believed that the Franciscan and Poor Clare communities had become too relaxed in their observance of poverty and enclosure and felt called to restore the original spirit of St. Clare of Assisi. With the support of Pope Benedict XIII, she began reforming the Poor Clares in 1406 AD. Colette travelled throughout France and neighbouring territories, founding and reforming monasteries according to a stricter observance centred on poverty, fasting, silence, enclosure, manual labour, and continual prayer. She established the Colettine Poor Clares, a reform branch of the Poor Clare Order, and eventually founded or reformed numerous monasteries across France, Belgium, and the surrounding regions. Colette also maintained a deep devotion to the Passion of Christ and the Eucharist and was regarded by those who knew her as a woman of profound prayer and spiritual insight. She endured considerable opposition and hardship during her reforming work but remained committed to restoring the simplicity and penitential spirit of Franciscan religious life.",

      death:
        "Colette Boylet died on March 6, 1447 AD, in Ghent, Belgium, after many years of religious reform and pastoral leadership. She had spent the greater part of her adult life travelling between monasteries, guiding communities, establishing new foundations, and encouraging religious women to embrace a life of poverty and contemplation. She was buried at the Colettine monastery in Ghent, where her tomb became a place of devotion. Her reputation for holiness had already spread widely during her lifetime, and devotion to her continued among the communities she had established.",

      legacy:
        "St. Colette is remembered as one of the great women reformers of the Franciscan tradition and as one of the most influential figures in the history of the Poor Clares. Her reform restored a stricter interpretation of Franciscan poverty and enclosure and helped preserve the contemplative vocation envisioned by St. Clare of Assisi. The communities founded or reformed by Colette became known as the Colettine Poor Clares and continued to spread throughout Europe after her death. Her spirituality placed particular emphasis on poverty, penance, silence, contemplation, Eucharistic devotion, and union with the suffering Christ. She is also traditionally invoked by women during pregnancy and childbirth and by those seeking help in family life. Her influence continues through the monasteries of Colettine Poor Clares that preserve the form of religious life she worked so tirelessly to establish.",

      canonization: "Colette Boylet was canonized by Pope Pius VII in 1807 AD.",
    },
