      spiritualLife:
        "As Bishop of Lucca, Anselm became a strong supporter of the Gregorian Reform, the movement within the Church that sought to restore clerical discipline, combat simony, strengthen the moral life of the clergy, and defend the Church's freedom from political interference. He was a close supporter of Pope Gregory VII and firmly defended the authority of the papacy during the Investiture Controversy. Anselm opposed the practice by which secular rulers attempted to appoint bishops and other Church officials, believing that ecclesiastical offices should be governed according to the authority and discipline of the Church. His commitment to reform brought him into conflict with powerful political interests. He eventually left Lucca because of opposition to his reforming policies and spent time in exile while continuing to support Gregory VII. Anselm remained loyal to the pope during the difficult conflict with Emperor Henry IV and became an important figure in the reforming party. He also worked to improve the discipline of religious communities and encouraged greater devotion and moral seriousness among both clergy and laity. His ministry reflected the broader Gregorian conviction that the Church needed to recover spiritual integrity before it could effectively fulfil its mission in the world.",

