        "Óscar Arnulfo Romero was born on August 15, 1917 AD, in Ciudad Barrios, El Salvador, the second of eight children in a modest Catholic family. From an early age, he showed a strong interest in the priesthood and entered the minor seminary in San Miguel when he was thirteen years old. He later continued his studies at the national seminary in San Salvador before being sent to Rome to study theology at the Pontifical Gregorian University. Romero was ordained a priest in Rome on April 4, 1942 AD, and initially intended to complete doctoral studies, but he was recalled to El Salvador before completing his doctorate. He returned home and began his priestly ministry in the Diocese of San Miguel, where he spent many years serving parishes, promoting Catholic organisations, and working with the poor. His quiet and disciplined character earned him a reputation for being deeply devoted to prayer, the Eucharist, and the teachings of the Church.",

      spiritualLife:
        "Romero served as a priest, seminary rector, auxiliary bishop, and later Bishop of Santiago de María before being appointed Archbishop of San Salvador in 1977 AD. His appointment initially surprised many people because he was regarded as a relatively conservative and reserved churchman. However, the murder of his close friend, the Jesuit priest Rutilio Grande, shortly after Romero became archbishop had a profound effect on him. Grande had worked closely with poor rural communities and was killed because of his ministry among them. Romero increasingly recognised the suffering being experienced by ordinary Salvadorans amid political repression, social inequality, and escalating violence. As archbishop, he became an increasingly outspoken defender of the poor and persecuted. Through his Sunday homilies, pastoral letters, and radio broadcasts, he denounced killings, disappearances, torture, and other abuses while calling both the government and armed groups to reject violence. Romero consistently presented his defence of human dignity as a consequence of the Gospel rather than as a political ideology. He insisted that the Church must stand alongside those who suffer and repeatedly called for conversion, reconciliation, peace, and respect for human life. His homilies were broadcast across El Salvador and became an important source of hope for people living amid fear and violence. He also established pastoral programmes to support the poor and documented abuses committed against civilians. Despite receiving death threats and facing intense opposition, Romero refused to abandon his ministry. He continued celebrating Mass, preaching the Gospel, and speaking publicly for the dignity of every human person. In one of his final homilies, he directly appealed to soldiers and members of the security forces to obey God's law rather than commands that required them to kill their fellow human beings. His growing identification with the suffering of the Salvadoran people eventually made him a target for those who opposed his message.",

      death:
        "Oscar Romero was assassinated on March 24, 1980 AD, while celebrating Mass in the chapel of the Hospital of Divine Providence in San Salvador. As he stood at the altar after giving his homily, a gunman shot him in the chest. Romero died shortly afterwards from his wounds. His assassination took place during a period of rapidly escalating political violence in El Salvador and became a defining moment in the country's history. His funeral several days later was attended by enormous crowds, but the ceremony itself was disrupted by violence and gunfire, resulting in further deaths and injuries. Romero's death shocked the Catholic Church and the wider world, and many Christians immediately regarded him as a martyr who had been killed because of his courageous defence of the Gospel and the dignity of the poor.",

