        "Lucy came under the influence of Cardinal Marco Antonio Barbarigo, Bishop of Montefiascone, who recognised her gifts and encouraged her vocation to Christian education. She was also influenced by the educational work of St. Rose Venerini, whose schools sought to provide girls with both religious instruction and practical education. Lucy became deeply committed to improving the education of women and girls, particularly those from poor families who had little access to formal schooling. She established schools throughout the Diocese of Montefiascone, where girls were taught Christian doctrine, reading, writing, domestic skills, and other subjects necessary for their lives. Her educational mission combined intellectual formation with the development of Christian virtue, seeking to form women who would strengthen their families and communities through faith. The community of teachers that developed around her eventually became the Religious Teachers Filippini. Lucy personally trained and guided the women who joined her work, insisting that teachers themselves should possess strong prayer lives and exemplary Christian character. Her ministry was particularly significant because education for girls was often neglected, and she believed that educating women was an important means of renewing Christian society.",

